# SNES Development Context

When working on assembly, patches, or ROM metadata in this repo:

1. Prefer the **gaia-snes** multi-root workspace (`c:/Work/gaia-snes.code-workspace`) so `gaia-knowledge` is indexed
2. Read `gaia-knowledge/curated/gaialabs/assembler-syntax.md` before editing `.asm`
3. Read `gaia-core/docs/patch-development.md` for patch structure, tag rules, and common pitfalls
4. IOG-specific docs: `docs/cop/index.md` (COP system overview + family links), `docs/actor-organization-analysis.md` (also in knowledge corpus after ingest)
5. Rebuild to validate: `npm run rebuild`

## Label rules

`$&` = same-bank (2 bytes), `$@` = cross-bank (3 bytes). Do not guess.

## COP register hazard

**X must = actor slot ID before every COP instruction.** If X is clobbered by loop counters or scratch code, wrap it in `PHX`/`PLX`. The COP dispatch uses X for actor context — a wrong X causes corrupt spawns, linked list damage, or crashes. See `gaia-core/docs/patch-development.md` §"COP Commands Require X = Actor Slot ID".

**$00/$01 is the actor re-entry address** (saved by SetEntryHere). In looping actors (SetEntryHere + RTL), never overwrite `$00` via `STA $0000` without save/restore. See `gaia-core/docs/patch-development.md` §"Actor Entry Point".

## Persistent DP hazard

DP `$80`–`$82` is the **collision tile map pointer** (`[$80], Y` in `tile_collision_physics.asm`). DP `$30`/`$34` are SPC transfer state, `$36` is the frame parity counter. Any patch/overlay code that uses these as scratch must save/restore engine low DP ($00-$94). See `gaia-core/docs/patch-development.md` §"Persistent Engine DP State Must Be Preserved".

## Changing notes and comments

Use `npm run merge -- <spec.json>` (dry-run first with `--dry-run`, then `npm run extract:lt` to verify). **Never** use `npm run ingest` or `npm run annotate`: they read the extracted `.asm` back into `notes/` and overwrite notes when `extracted/` is stale or line-tracked. Process: `gaia-core/docs/notes-and-comments.md` §"Notes Merge". (`npm run ingest:all` in gaia-knowledge is the unrelated RAG corpus refresh.)

## Database files

- `us/blocks.json` — block/part structure (engine auto-discovers unmapped pieces)
- `us/overrides.json` — per-address register state and type corrections (do **not** use `"name"` key — deprecated; use `names/bankNN.json` for naming)
- `db-us/names/bankNN.json` — human-readable address labels, **one file per ROM bank** (bank = floor(address / 65536); keys are decimal addresses, sorted). A new bank file must also be imported in `src/names.ts`. See `gaia-core/docs/notes-and-comments.md` §"Names"
- `db-us/transforms.json` — per-block regex replacements applied during extraction (e.g., button mask changes)

All four are needed for the complete picture (names are the per-bank files under `db-us/names/`). See `gaia-knowledge/curated/gaialabs/reference-model.md`.

## Label override hazards

**Flat namespace:** All labels (`{`, `:`, `[`, `|`) create separate `AsmBlock` entries in a flat array. Braces are cosmetic. `Label!` replaces exactly ONE block — internal colon labels persist unchanged (sub-label orphaning). Override sub-labels too, or use `transforms.json` for simple constant changes. See `gaia-core/docs/patch-development.md` §"Sub-Label Orphaning".

